  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Cool this afternoon with lake effect rain showers \ngradually ending by this evening.\n\nA deep trough centered over eastern Canada will continue to support \nmoist cyclonic flow over the eastern Great Lakes through early this \nevening, combining with cool air aloft to support a wealth of \nstratocumulus. \n\nThe airmass is cool enough to support some lake response, with a \npersistent band of lake effect showers today at the southeast corner \nof Lake Ontario. These showers will become less organized through \nthe rest of the afternoon from the negative impact of diurnal \nboundary layer mixing and exit of deeper synoptic scale moisture. A \nfew additional scattered showers will develop through the afternoon \nand early evening east of Lake Ontario from upslope flow and diurnal \nsteepening of low level lapse rates. The last of these showers \nshould end by mid evening. A few scattered showers will also \ncontinue across the higher terrain of the western Southern Tier from \na combination of lake instability and upslope flow, with these \nending by late afternoon or early evening.\n\nHigh pressure will build east across the Great Lakes overnight \nthrough Thursday. Skies will clear from west to east tonight with \nthe arrival of drier air and subsidence. It will be a cool night, \nwith lows in the low to mid 50s on the lake plains and mid 40s in \nthe Southern Tier valleys and Lewis County. \n\nKey Message 2...Dry weather returns Thursday and continues through \nSaturday with a day to day warming trend.\n\nHigh pressure will build east across the Great Lakes Thursday, then \nlinger over the eastern Great Lakes and New England Friday through \nSaturday. This will allow for a nice stretch of dry and benign \nweather to close out the week. The next trough will dig into the \nGreat Lakes by Sunday, bringing a chance of a few scattered showers \nand thunderstorms.\n\nThe onset of weak warm advection and more in the way of sunshine \nwill allow temperatures to recover to the mid 70s for lower \nelevations and around 70 across higher terrain Thursday afternoon \nafter a cool start to the day. Steady warm advection will continue \nto support a day to day warming trend through the end of the week. \nHighs for lower elevations will reach the lower 80s Friday, and mid \n80s by Saturday.",
